<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="CcpClient.Desktop.Views.Pages.PlayPage">
  <!--
    The Play page and its DTRH hero card (wpf-surface-reachability.md §3 @ 7527243e7 and §8.5; source at
    Views/Tabs/PlayTabView.xaml). Hop 2 of the port's flagship route: the rail door NAVIGATES
    here, and the one window-opening button lives on this page — WPF's one-entry rule
    (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1007,1036).

    THE CARD TAKES THE CLICK. Nothing here is ever disabled. WPF's lock band is a scrim, not a
    curtain: IsHitTestVisible="False" so the press passes straight through and TierGate.DemandLab
    inside the handler does the refusing (PlayTabView.xaml:508-512, style :251-258, and the
    band's own comment at :503-506 — "a band that swallowed the click would be a dead end").
    The one genuinely disabled part in WPF is the checkbox pair, and only because it writes
    settings through a binding with no handler to refuse in (MainWindow/MainWindow.PlayTab.cs:88-91).

    WHAT IS OMITTED, AND WHY (divergences §10 D18, D19). WPF's card also carries "Announcements"
    and "3D game (recommended)" checkboxes (PlayTabView.xaml:488,494) and a NEW pill (:432).
    Neither setting is ported, and unticking the 3D box selects a classic WPF descent the port
    does not have — so rendering them would be dead dials, which is the greyed control that
    swallows the gesture (the §9 D7 rule). They are absent, not disabled.
  -->
  <StackPanel Margin="20" Spacing="4">
    <TextBlock Classes="page-title" Text="Play" />
    <!-- The page's own live sub-line (§8.5). -->
    <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Games, modes, and the deep end." />

    <Border Classes="hero" Margin="0,16,0,0">
      <Panel>
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="10">
            <!-- The tier livery, top-left on this hero exactly as in WPF
                 (PlayTabView.xaml:398-411, Tier="2"). The words are the badge ART's words
                 (Controls/TierBadge.cs:21 — "BASIC SUBJECT" / "PRIME SUBJECT" are baked into
                 the PNGs, which is why the feature adds no localisation keys). The literal
                 string "TIER 2" appears nowhere in the running app and must not appear here.
                 It is a PRICE TAG on the feature, never a claim about the person reading it:
                 the badge "reads the tier, never decides it" (TierBadge.cs:24). -->
            <Border Classes="tier-badge" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
              <TextBlock x:Name="TierBadgeText"
                         Classes="tier-badge-text"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TierBadgeText"
                         Text="PRIME SUBJECT" />
            </Border>

            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:426, emoji-stripped (the §9 D8 rule: the app strips emoji
                 mod-aware, MainWindow/MainWindow.UiUpdates.cs:101,124). §8.5 of the evidence
                 doc read this as "THE RABBIT HOLE" from a capture in which the onboarding card
                 and the badge occluded the left of the title; that reading is corrected in the
                 doc by this packet. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="HeroTitle"
                       Classes="hero-title"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HeroTitle"
                       Text="DOWN THE RABBIT HOLE" />

            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:435, verbatim. Deliberately never localized upstream. -->
            <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" MaxWidth="520" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                       Text="it's right there. it's always been right there. bubbles drift up carrying flashes, videos, overlays. snap them in time or let them trigger you. stack streaks, accept mantras, sink deeper." />
          </StackPanel>

          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="24,0,0,0" Spacing="8">
            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:455,458 — the pink primary, and its tooltip verbatim. -->
            <Button x:Name="FallInButton"
                    Classes="hero-primary"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FallInButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="FALL IN"
                    ToolTip.Tip="you were always going to."
                    Content="FALL IN" />
            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:468-469 — outlined, skips the picker, reuses the last slot. -->
            <Button x:Name="QuickDropButton"
                    Classes="hero-secondary"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="QuickDropButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Quick Drop"
                    ToolTip.Tip="skip the dollhouse. fall straight in with your saved settings"
                    Content="Quick Drop" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        <!-- The refusal band. WPF's colours: scrim #A8120A1E ("~66% so the card art still reads
             through it. Seeing what you are missing is the entire job." PlayTabView.xaml:247-248),
             tier-2 rim #FFB47BFF (:245), corner 15 = the card's 16 minus its 1px border (:252).

             IsHitTestVisible="False" is declared on the ELEMENT, not tucked into a style, because
             it is the load-bearing attribute of the whole surface (PlayTabView.xaml:257): a band
             that took the click would swallow the gesture and tell the user nothing.

             DIVERGENCE §10 D16: WPF paints its band from the tier it already knows, BEFORE any
             click, and the band reads "Lab only". The port cannot know a tier without an async
             read, and a band painted from a cached read would advertise a state nobody
             re-checked — so this band appears when a press is refused and carries the refusal
             itself. §10 D17: WPF's refusal is additionally an 8-second Warning toast with a
             "See tiers" action (Services/TierGate.cs:133). THIS PORT NOW HAS A TOAST SYSTEM
             (Views/ToastHost, census #54) AND STILL DOES NOT USE IT HERE, which is a decision
             with a measurement behind it rather than the old "we have no toast".

             (1) The ACTION cannot be built: it opens App Info &amp; Data and this port has no
             such page. A "See tiers" button with nowhere to go is a dead control, so the route
             is named in WORDS inside the message instead (DtrhGate.UpgradeRoute) and the gap is
             admitted there.

             (2) The ANNOUNCEMENT was built, measured and taken back out. The shell's toast
             surface is docked top-right (MainWindow.axaml, ToastLayer) and a refusal toast lands
             at 724,40 360x153 in the shell's own 1100x760 window — which fully covers FALL IN at
             883,131 172x46 and clips the top of Quick Drop at 905,185 150x28. A press at FALL
             IN's centre then hit-tests to the toast's own Border (a toast body carries a
             background and captures its own clicks, exactly as upstream's do:
             MainWindow.xaml:3212-3216) and the button's Click never fires. That takes away the
             one thing this card guarantees — "a gated press must ARRIVE"
             (PlayTabView.xaml:503-506) — in order to say a sentence the plate below is already
             saying, durably and without covering anything. The measurement is pinned by
             TierRefusalRouteHeadlessTests.

             So the row stays open, and its close condition is now specific: an App Info page
             gives the refusal a real destination, OR a toast placed clear of this card's buttons
             makes the announcement free. Neither is this packet's.

             TWO LAYERS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS LOAD-BEARING (§10 D23, found by a headed
             capture). WPF's scrim carries only a glyph and ONE short no-wrap line, and its own
             comment says why: "The pitch is the toast the click raises, which carries the
             'See tiers' button - not this" (PlayTabView.xaml:270-273). The prose lives on the
             toast, which is an opaque surface with its own ground. The port has no toast, so
             the prose lands here — and prose on a 66%-alpha scrim composites straight through
             onto the card's own title and blurb, which is illegible however correct the words
             are. So the message gets a PLATE: its own opaque panel inside the band. The scrim
             stays at WPF's ~66% ("so the card art still reads through it. Seeing what you are
             missing is the entire job", :247-248) and still shows the badge, the buttons and
             the card's edges around the plate. Making the scrim itself opaque would have
             bought legibility by destroying exactly the quality that alpha value exists for. -->
        <Border x:Name="GateBand"
                Classes="lock-band"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                IsVisible="False">
          <Border x:Name="GateBandPlate"
                  Classes="lock-plate"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GateBandPlate"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Margin="16"
                  MaxWidth="600">
            <StackPanel Spacing="8">
              <TextBlock x:Name="GateBandTitle"
                         Classes="lock-title"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GateBandTitle"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                         Text="" />
              <!-- Left-aligned, not centred: WPF centres ONE short line, and this is three
                   sentences. Centred prose is harder to scan, and the message is the most
                   load-bearing text on the surface. -->
              <TextBlock x:Name="GateBandText"
                         Classes="lock-text"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GateBandText"
                         TextAlignment="Left"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </Border>

        <!-- THE FAILURE SURFACE, and it is deliberately NOT the band above.

             WPF wraps the whole DTRH handler and shows a blocking MessageBox titled "Down the
             Rabbit Hole" reading "Couldn't start Down the Rabbit Hole:" plus the exception message,
             with MessageBoxImage.Warning (MainWindow/MainWindow.Lab.cs:266-271, and the identical
             catch on Quick Start at :333-338). The port had no such surface at all: PlayPage fired
             `_ = dtrh.FallInAsync();` and a throw from the descent became an unobserved task
             exception — logged by the panic hook at some later GC (CcpClient.Desktop/Program.cs:313) and never shown.

             WHY A BAND AND NOT A DIALOG. The port has no dialog service, so a modal here would be
             a new window class whose every user-visible property (modality, activation, focus
             return, z-order) is presentation-class evidence a headless frame cannot discharge; and
             the page already has an honest in-page idiom for saying something out loud. WPF's
             dialog is dismissed and the card is live again, which is what a hit-test-transparent
             band gives without a modal loop.

             WHY IT LOOKS DIFFERENT FROM THE BAND ABOVE, on purpose. That band means "we could not
             determine your entitlement". If a fault rendered identically, the user would be taught
             that a broken app and an unknown subscription are the same event. So this is a
             different ELEMENT (not the same one re-tinted), in a different livery (amber — the
             severity MessageBoxImage.Warning carries as a glyph, carried here as colour), under a
             different headline (WPF's own "Couldn't start …" words), and its text says in prose
             that nothing was refused. Divergence §13 D41.

             The two are mutually exclusive: raising this hides the gate band, and the next gate
             decision hides this. Same IsHitTestVisible="False" rule, for the same reason as above
             and for WPF's: a dismissed dialog leaves a live card, so the retry must arrive. -->
        <Border x:Name="FaultBand"
                Classes="fault-band"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                IsVisible="False">
          <Border x:Name="FaultBandPlate"
                  Classes="fault-plate"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FaultBandPlate"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Margin="16"
                  MaxWidth="600">
            <StackPanel Spacing="8">
              <TextBlock x:Name="FaultBandTitle"
                         Classes="fault-title"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FaultBandTitle"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="FaultBandText"
                         Classes="fault-text"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FaultBandText"
                         TextAlignment="Left"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </Border>
      </Panel>
    </Border>

    <!-- THE GOON DOOR. Upstream's Goon card sits on this same tab
         (Views/Tabs/PlayTabView.xaml:550, opened by BtnStartGoon_Click at
         MainWindow/MainWindow.Lab.cs:198) and carries NO lock band, because joining is free by
         design: ":547-549" says the two paid rungs are named in a sub-line, "never a padlock
         over an open door". This door is ungated for the same reason and is not a gate port.

         WHAT THE DOOR SAYS IS WHAT IT DOES. It offers Practice and says so in its own label and
         sub-line, because that is the whole of what this build has (goon-game-census.md 7.1 -
         the buildable unit). The four owner-gated siblings are named on the host window's
         refusal rail, typed, the moment it opens - never here as a teaser and never as a
         "coming soon". -->
    <Border Classes="hero" Margin="0,16,0,0">
      <StackPanel Spacing="10">
        <TextBlock x:Name="GoonTitle"
                   Classes="hero-title"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GoonTitle"
                   Text="GOON GAME" />
        <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   Text="a duel of endurance against a scripted opponent: throw flashes, videos, subliminals and bubbles, build heat, and settle it in sudden death." />
        <!-- The honest sub-line, in the door itself. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="GoonScopeLine"
                   Classes="page-blurb"
                   MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GoonScopeLine"
                   Text="This build plays Practice only. Hosting, joining, voice notes and sending your own media are not in it, and the window says exactly what each one is missing." />
        <Button x:Name="GoonPracticeButton"
                Classes="hero-primary"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GoonPracticeButton"
                AutomationProperties.Name="PRACTICE"
                ToolTip.Tip="a complete duel, against the scripted opponent"
                Content="PRACTICE" />
        <!-- The launch fault surface, deliberately its own element: "the app broke" is not a
             refusal, and the refusals are not faults (the same rule the DTRH card's two bands
             follow above). -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="GoonFaultText"
                   Classes="fault-text"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GoonFaultText"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   MaxWidth="640"
                   IsVisible="False"
                   Text="" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <!-- THE ARCADEMY, AND IT SHIPS DARK. Upstream's strip sits in this same tab's MORE zone
         (Views/Tabs/PlayTabView.xaml:1312) with Visibility="Collapsed" as its DEFAULT, and
         RefreshPlayCards re-asserts that from ArcademyHostService.DoorAvailable on every repaint
         (MainWindow/MainWindow.PlayTab.cs:106-112). Both halves are ported. The RE-ASSERT is the
         load-bearing one and it is what a fact holds: it runs in PlayPage's constructor and again
         on every mount - the port's repaint, since the page is mounted each time the Play door is
         chosen - so a stray IsVisible written here or anywhere else cannot survive a navigation.
         The markup default below is the belt to that brace, the value the strip carries if the
         derivation is ever removed, which is the same pairing upstream ships.

         There is NO reveal switch. ArcademyDoor.Available is a static readonly false with no
         override seam, no argument and no environment variable, so nothing in a shipped build
         can turn this on - and Attend refuses on that same flag anyway, because the code path
         that opens the door has to be the one that can say no (ArcademyHostService.cs:133-141).

         The strip's shape is upstream's: a title, a blurb (verbatim, :1332-1333), a T2 sign, and
         a single "Attend" button (:1353-1359). No hero art, for upstream's own reason at
         :1301-1305 - there is none for it yet, and a card built around a missing image is a
         failure mode this wall already knows about. -->
    <StackPanel x:Name="ArcademyEntry"
                Margin="0,16,0,0"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ArcademyEntry"
                IsVisible="False">
      <Border Classes="hero">
        <StackPanel Spacing="10">
          <!-- The badge art's own words for tier 2 (Controls/TierBadge.cs:21), never the literal
               "TIER 2": it is a price tag on the feature, not a claim about the reader. -->
          <Border Classes="tier-badge" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock Classes="tier-badge-text"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ArcademyTierBadgeText"
                       Text="PRIME SUBJECT" />
          </Border>
          <TextBlock x:Name="ArcademyTitle"
                     Classes="hero-title"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ArcademyTitle"
                     Text="THE ARCADEMY" />
          <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:1332, verbatim. -->
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                     Text="three classes a day. simple little games made hard by everything else on your screen. show up, get graded, keep the streak." />
          <Button x:Name="ArcademyAttendButton"
                  Classes="hero-primary"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ArcademyAttendButton"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="Attend"
                  ToolTip.Tip="today's timetable is waiting."
                  Content="Attend" />
          <!-- The tier bar's refusal, when there is one. Never disabled and never a curtain: the
               press ARRIVES and ArcademyLaunch does the refusing (:1358 -> MainWindow.Lab.cs:310
               -> Launch's own gates). -->
          <TextBlock x:Name="ArcademyGateText"
                     Classes="fault-text"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ArcademyGateText"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     MaxWidth="640"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     Text="" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
